Most Useful Kubernetes Commands: Complete Guide with Concepts
Understanding Core Kubernetes Concepts First
Before diving into commands, it's important to understand what you're managing. Kubernetes is an orchestration platform that manages containerized applications across clusters of machines. The main resources you'll work with include Pods (smallest deployable units), Deployments (managing replicas of pods), Services (networking), and ConfigMaps/Secrets (configuration management).

1. Configuration & Context Management
These commands establish your connection to Kubernetes clusters and manage which cluster you're working with.

Copy# View your current configuration
kubectl config view

# List all available contexts (cluster configurations)
kubectl config get-contexts

# Show your current context
kubectl config current-context

# Switch to a different cluster context
kubectl config use-context my-cluster-name

# Set a specific namespace as default for current context
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=my-namespace
Why use these? When working with multiple Kubernetes clusters, you need to ensure you're executing commands against the right cluster. Context switching prevents accidental deployments to production.

2. Viewing Resources (The GET Commands)
Getting information about your cluster's resources is fundamental. These commands retrieve data without modifying anything.

Copy# List all pods in the current namespace
kubectl get pods

# List all pods across all namespaces
kubectl get pods --all-namespaces
# Shorthand: kubectl get pods -A

# List pods with detailed information
kubectl get pods -o wide

# Get detailed YAML for a specific pod
kubectl get pod my-pod -o yaml

# List all services in the cluster
kubectl get services

# List all deployments
kubectl get deployments

# Sort resources by a specific field
kubectl get pods --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp

# Filter pods by label selector
kubectl get pods -l app=myapp
Why use these? These are your primary tools for monitoring what's running in your cluster. The -o flag (output format) gives you flexibility in how you view data.

3. Describing Resources (Getting Deep Details)
When you need more comprehensive information about a specific resource, describe provides a human-readable summary including events and status conditions.

Copy# Get detailed information about a specific pod
kubectl describe pod my-pod

# Describe a deployment
kubectl describe deployment my-deployment

# Describe a node
kubectl describe node my-node
Why use these? When troubleshooting, describe shows you events, conditions, and status that indicate what went wrong. The get command shows the configuration, while describe shows the actual state and what happened to reach that state.

4. Creating Resources
There are two approaches to creating resources: declarative (recommended) and imperative.

Copy# Declarative approach - create from YAML file (recommended)
kubectl apply -f my-manifest.yaml

# Create from multiple files
kubectl apply -f ./dir

# Create from a URL
kubectl apply -f https://example.com/manifest.yaml

# Imperative approach - create resources directly with flags
kubectl create deployment my-app --image=nginx

# Create a job
kubectl create job my-job --image=busybox -- echo "Hello"

# Create a cronjob
kubectl create cronjob my-cronjob --image=busybox --schedule="0 0 * * *" -- echo "Scheduled"

# Create a pod interactively
kubectl run my-pod --image=nginx
Why use these? The declarative approach (kubectl apply) is preferred for production because it tracks infrastructure as code. Imperative commands are useful for quick testing but shouldn't be used for persistent configurations.

5. Updating and Managing Resources
These commands modify existing resources in your cluster.

Copy# Update an image in a deployment (rolling update)
kubectl set image deployment/my-app container=new-image:v2

# Scale a deployment to a specific number of replicas
kubectl scale deployment my-app --replicas=3

# Add a label to a pod
kubectl label pods my-pod environment=production

# Remove a label
kubectl label pods my-pod environment-

# Add an annotation
kubectl annotate pods my-pod description="This is my pod"

# Edit a resource in your default editor
kubectl edit pod my-pod

# Patch a resource (make quick changes)
kubectl patch pod my-pod -p '{"spec":{"activeDeadlineSeconds":120}}'
Why use these? These commands let you modify resources without redeploying. Rolling updates gradually replace old pod instances with new ones, preventing downtime.

6. Rollout Management (Deployment Updates)
When you update a deployment, Kubernetes manages the transition. These commands control that process.

Copy# Check the history of rollouts for a deployment
kubectl rollout history deployment/my-app

# Check the status of an ongoing rollout
kubectl rollout status deployment/my-app

# Rollback to the previous version
kubectl rollout undo deployment/my-app

# Rollback to a specific revision
kubectl rollout undo deployment/my-app --to-revision=2

# Restart a deployment (recreate all pods)
kubectl rollout restart deployment/my-app
Why use these? Rollouts allow you to deploy new versions safely. If something goes wrong, you can quickly rollback to the previous version without manual intervention.

7. Debugging: Viewing Logs
Logs help you understand what's happening inside your containers and troubleshoot issues.

Copy# View logs from a pod
kubectl logs my-pod

# Stream logs in real-time (like tail -f)
kubectl logs -f my-pod

# View logs from a previous container instance
kubectl logs my-pod --previous

# View logs from a specific container in a multi-container pod
kubectl logs my-pod -c my-container

# View logs from all pods with a specific label
kubectl logs -l app=myapp

# View logs from a deployment
kubectl logs deploy/my-deployment
Why use these? Logs are your first debugging tool. They show application output, errors, and warnings that help identify issues.

8. Executing Commands Inside Containers
Sometimes you need to run commands inside a running container for debugging or inspection.

Copy# Execute a command in a pod
kubectl exec my-pod -- ls /

# Open an interactive shell inside a pod
kubectl exec -it my-pod -- /bin/sh

# Execute a command in a specific container (multi-container pods)
kubectl exec my-pod -c my-container -- env

# Run a one-off pod for debugging
kubectl run -it busybox --image=busybox -- sh
Why use these? Interactive access lets you investigate what's happening inside your container. This is essential when logs don't tell you everything.

9. Networking: Port Forwarding and Service Exposure
These commands help you access services and test connectivity.

Copy# Forward a local port to a pod's port
kubectl port-forward pod/my-pod 8080:8000

# Forward a local port to a service
kubectl port-forward svc/my-service 8080:80

# Expose a deployment as a service
kubectl expose deployment my-app --port=80 --target-port=8000 --type=LoadBalancer

# View all services
kubectl get svc
Why use these? Port forwarding lets you test applications without exposing them to the internet. Services provide stable endpoints for accessing your applications.

10. Deletion: Cleaning Up Resources
Remove resources you no longer need to avoid wasting resources and money.

Copy# Delete a specific pod
kubectl delete pod my-pod

# Delete a deployment (and its pods)
kubectl delete deployment my-app

# Delete all pods in the current namespace
kubectl delete pods --all

# Delete with a grace period (time to shut down gracefully)
kubectl delete pod my-pod --grace-period=30

# Force delete immediately
kubectl delete pod my-pod --force

# Delete multiple resource types
kubectl delete pods,services -l app=myapp
Why use these? Proper deletion ensures resources are cleaned up correctly. The grace period allows applications to shut down gracefully, saving data and closing connections properly.

11. Cluster Information and Diagnostics
These commands give you visibility into your cluster's health and structure.

Copy# Get cluster information
kubectl cluster-info

# View all nodes in the cluster
kubectl get nodes

# Describe a node to see its resources and conditions
kubectl describe node my-node

# Show resource usage (requires metrics-server installed)
kubectl top nodes
kubectl top pod

# Mark a node as unschedulable (for maintenance)
kubectl cordon my-node

# Drain a node (move pods before maintenance)
kubectl drain my-node

# Mark a node as schedulable again
kubectl uncordon my-node
Why use these? These commands help you manage cluster infrastructure. Cordoning and draining are essential for safe node maintenance.

12. Advanced Output Formatting
Kubernetes provides powerful output options to extract exactly the data you need.

Copy# Output as JSON
kubectl get pods -o json

# Output as YAML
kubectl get pods -o yaml

# Custom columns
kubectl get pods -o=custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,RESTARTS:.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount

# JSONPath extraction
kubectl get pods -o jsonpath='{.items[*].metadata.name}'

# Wide output with additional columns
kubectl get pods -o wide
Why use these? Advanced formatting lets you extract specific information and integrate kubectl with scripts or other tools.

13. Essential Best Practices
Use these patterns in your daily work:

Copy# Always specify namespaces for safety
kubectl get pods -n production

# Use labels for organization
kubectl label nodes node1 disk=ssd
kubectl get pods -l tier=frontend

# Use dry-run to test before applying
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml --dry-run=client

# Check diffs before applying
kubectl diff -f deployment.yaml

# Use resource quotas to prevent resource exhaustion
kubectl describe quota -n my-namespace
Quick Command Reference Table
Task	Command
View pods	kubectl get pods
Describe pod details	kubectl describe pod NAME
View logs	kubectl logs POD_NAME
Execute command	kubectl exec -it POD_NAME -- COMMAND
Create resource	kubectl apply -f file.yaml
Update image	kubectl set image deployment/NAME container=IMAGE
Scale deployment	kubectl scale deployment/NAME --replicas=N
Rollback	kubectl rollout undo deployment/NAME
Delete resource	kubectl delete pod/service/deployment NAME
Port forward	kubectl port-forward pod/NAME 8080:8000
These commands form the foundation of Kubernetes management. Mastering them makes you effective in managing containerized applications at scale.
